window.docContents[87].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Induced-representation-Ind_H___G-V/#Chapter5___Definition5___8___1":{"contents":"Definition 5.8.1. If G is a group, H \\subset G, and V is a representation of H, then the induced representation \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G V, is the representation of G with\n\n\\operatorname{Ind}_H^G V = \\{f : G \\to V \\mid f(hx) = \\rho_V(h) f(x) \\ \\forall x \\in G, h \\in H\\}\n\nand the action g(f)(x) = f(xg) \\forall g \\in G.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.8. Induced representations\tInduced representation Ind_H^G V","header":"Induced representation Ind_H^G V","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Induced-representation-Ind_H___G-V/#Chapter5___Definition5___8___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity/Tensor-products-over-k-algebras-framework-for-Ind-and-Res-_LPAR_continues-to-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Problem5___10___2":{"contents":"Problem 5.10.2. The purpose of this problem is to understand the notions of restricted and induced representations as part of a more advanced framework. This framework is the notion of tensor products over k-algebras. In particular, this understanding will lead us to a\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.10. Frobenius reciprocity\tTensor products over k-algebras framework for Ind and Res (continues to missing page)","header":"Tensor products over k-algebras framework for Ind and Res (continues to missing page)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity/Tensor-products-over-k-algebras-framework-for-Ind-and-Res-_LPAR_continues-to-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Problem5___10___2"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4-using-Hom-decomposition/#Chapter3___Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3___1___4":{"contents":"Here is an alternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4.We thank B. Poonen for this argument\n\nBy Remark 3.1.3, if V = \\bigoplus_X V_X \\otimes X and U = \\bigoplus_X U_X \\otimes X for some vector spaces V_X and U_X, then we have a natural isomorphism\n\n\\operatorname{Hom}_A(V, U) \\cong \\prod_X \\operatorname{Hom}(V_X, U_X).\n\nNow let f : V \\to U correspond to the tuple (f_X : V_X \\to U_X). Then f is injective (respectively surjective, an isomorphism) if and only if all the f_X are.\n\nNow, suppose V = \\bigoplus_{i \\in I} V_i with V_i irreducible, and f : V \\to U is a surjective homomorphism.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\tAlternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4 using Hom decomposition","header":"Alternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4 using Hom decomposition","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4-using-Hom-decomposition/#Chapter3___Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3___1___4"}});
